@page "/Guides/GettingStarted"
@using RocketEngineDocs.Components

<h3>Getting Started</h3>

<p>
    Updated for v0.2.0  
    This guide walks you through creating a minimal Rocket Engine scene:  
    a window, a camera, a scene, and a simple renderable object.
</p>

<h3>Requirements</h3>
<ul>
    <li>.NET 9.0</li>
    <li>OpenTK (included through RERL)</li>
    <li>A GPU that supports OpenGL 4.6+</li>
</ul>

<h3>Installation</h3>

<p>Install via .NET CLI:</p>
<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
dotnet add package RocketEngine.RCS --version 0.2.0
dotnet add package RocketEngine.RERL --version 0.2.0
")" />

<p>Or install via NuGet Package Manager:</p>
<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
RocketEngine.RCS Version 0.2.0
RocketEngine.RERL Version 0.2.0
")" />

<p>You may also download the <code>.nupkg</code> files from the release page.</p>

<h3>Creating the Window</h3>

<p>
    Rocket Engine uses OpenTK for windowing.  
    Start by creating a <code>Game</code> class that inherits from <code>GameWindow</code>.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
using OpenTK.Windowing.Common;
using OpenTK.Windowing.Desktop;

static void Main(string[] args)
{
    var nativeSettings = new NativeWindowSettings
    {
        Title = ""Rocket Engine | Getting Started"",
        Size = new(1280, 720)
    };

    using var game = new Game(GameWindowSettings.Default, nativeSettings);
    game.Run();
}
")" />

<p>Then define the <code>Game</code> class:</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
public class Game(GameWindowSettings gws, NativeWindowSettings nws)
    : GameWindow(gws, nws)
{
    protected override void OnLoad()
    {
        base.OnLoad();
    }

    protected override void OnUpdateFrame(FrameEventArgs args)
    {
        base.OnUpdateFrame(args);
    }

    protected override void OnRenderFrame(FrameEventArgs args)
    {
        base.OnRenderFrame(args);
    }

    protected override void OnResize(ResizeEventArgs e)
    {
        base.OnResize(e);
    }
}
")" />

<h3>Initializing Rocket Engine</h3>

<p>
    Rocket Engine has two main systems:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>RCS</strong>: Entity/Component system (Rocket Control System)</li>
    <li><strong>RERL</strong>: Rendering system (Rocket Engine Rendering Library)</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Before rendering anything, you must tell RERL which window and camera to use.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
Camera _camera = new Camera();

protected override void OnLoad()
{
    base.OnLoad();

    // Set up projection, I prefer FovX so I will will be using that for this guide.
    _camera.SetProjectionFovXInDegrees(
        90,
        Size.X / (float)Size.Y,
        0.1f,
        100f
    );

    // Register window & camera with RERL
    RERL_Core.SetCamera(_camera);
    RERL_Core.SetGameWindow(this);
    RERL_Core.Load();
}
")" />

<h3>Creating a Scene</h3>

<p>
    RCS organizes your world into scenes.  
    A scene contains entities, and entities contain components.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
RCS_Core.Scene _scene;

protected override void OnLoad()
{
    base.OnLoad();

    _scene = new RCS_Core.Scene(""Main"");

    RCS_Core.AddScene(_scene); // Registers a scene to RCS.
    RCS_Core.SetActiveScene(""Main""); // There can only be one scene active at a time, active scene's get update calls. (And future under the hood stuff.)
    RCS_Core.LoadActiveScene(); // Calls a load for all entities/components in the active scene.
}
")" />

<h3>Adding a Camera</h3>

<p>
    You can use either the raw <code>Camera</code> class or the <code>CameraComponent</code>.  
    For now, we’ll use the component so the camera follows the entity’s transform.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
// In OnLoad()
var cameraEntity = new Entity(""Camera"")
    .AddComponent<CameraComponent>();

_scene.AddEntity(cameraEntity);
")" />

<p>
    When the window resizes, update the projection:
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
protected override void OnResize(ResizeEventArgs e)
{
    base.OnResize(e);

    var cam = cameraEntity.GetComponent<CameraComponent>();
    cam.SetProjectionFovXInDegrees(
        90,
        Size.X / (float)Size.Y,
        0.1f,
        100f
    );

    RERL_Core.Resize();
}
")" />

<h3>Rendering a Simple Object</h3>

<p>
    To render something, you need:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>A mesh (from <code>ModelLoader</code>)</li>
    <li>A shader</li>
    <li>A <code>ModelRenderer</code> component</li>
</ul>

<p>Here’s a basic cube:</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
var cube = new Entity(""Cube"")
    .AddComponent(new ModelRenderer()
        .AttachModel(ModelLoader.CubeMesh) // Cube model that comes with RERL.
        .AttachShader(RERL_Core.GetPrelightShader())); // The default rendering shader, there will be another guide in the future that touches on this more.

cube.Transform.Position = new Vector3(0, 1, -3);

_scene.AddEntity(cube);
")" />

<h3>Lights</h3>

<p>
    Lights are optional, but most scenes look better with at least one.  
    Here’s a simple point light:
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
var light = new Entity(""Light"")
    .AddComponent(
        LightBuilder.Point()
            .Radius(6)
            .Intensity(3)
            .Color(new Vector3(1, 0.95f, 0.8f))
            .Build()
    );

light.Transform.Position = new Vector3(0, 2, 0);

_scene.AddEntity(light);
")" />

<h3>Updating and Rendering</h3>

<p>
    RCS updates your scene.  
    RERL renders it.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@((_) => @"
protected override void OnUpdateFrame(FrameEventArgs args)
{
    base.OnUpdateFrame(args);
    RCS_Core.UpdateActiveScene(args.Time);
}

protected override void OnRenderFrame(FrameEventArgs args)
{
    base.OnRenderFrame(args);
    RERL_Core.RenderFrame(args);
}
")" />

<h3>That’s the Basics</h3>

<p>
    At this point you have:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>A window</li>
    <li>A camera</li>
    <li>A scene</li>
    <li>A renderable object</li>
    <li>A light</li>
</ul>

